<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheontae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1097">1097</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Korean Tiantai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cheontae is the Korean form of the Tiantai school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, which centres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a>. It was established as a separate order in Goryeo in 1097 by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uicheon">Uicheon</a>, who sought to reconcile doctrinal study with meditation and to unite the competing Buddhist schools. Revived in the twentieth century, it is today one of the largest Buddhist orders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheontae.jpg" alt="Cheontae" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kernbeisser (a flickr user) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Joseon_Dynasty-Gilt-bronze_seated_Avalokitesvara_at_Beopjusa_Temple-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chinese Tiantai school was founded in 594 by the monk Zhiyi under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. Its teachings reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> earlier, but Cheontae became an independent school only in the Goryeo period, through Daegak Guksa Uicheon (1055–1101), a royal prince and Buddhist scholar, who promoted it to bridge the gap between the doctrinal Gyo schools and the meditative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cheontae holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> to be the culmination of the Buddha's teaching. Its doctrine rests on three insights: that all phenomena are fundamentally empty, that they have a provisional or conventional existence, and that all things are at once empty and provisionally real. Everyday sensory experience is therefore an expression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> and a path to enlightenment, a view reflected in the colourful Dancheong painting of Korean temples, which contrasts with the minimalist style of Japanese Zen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Under Goryeo, Cheontae gained institutional recognition alongside the Jogye school, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was organised under the Five Orders and Two Schools, the two schools being the Jogye and Cheontae sects. Its influence waned under the Joseon dynasty, whose rulers sought to reunify the Buddhist schools: by 1424 Cheontae had been absorbed into the Chongnam sect, and later into the Seon-dominated structure known as Seon-Gyo Yangjong, the Two Schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> and Doctrinal Buddhism.</p><p>In the modern period Park Sangwol, also known as Sangwol Wongak Daejo, founded the present Korean Buddhist Cheontae Order, which regards Zhiyi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uicheon">Uicheon</a> and Park Sangwol as its spiritual forebears. Its headquarters are at Guinsa Temple near Danyang in Chungcheongbuk-do, built of concrete unlike the usual Buddhist temples, and the order runs Geumgang University. A foundation of the order was established on 16 April 1991 to carry on its education, the care of its temples, translation and welfare work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cheontae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great monasteries owned land and slaves, the monk Uicheon founded the <b>Cheontae</b> school, Jinul reformed Seon (Zen) Buddhism, and the state festivals of Yeondeunghoe and Palgwanhoe combined Buddhist ritual with native cults.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
